<!--
  about.ejs
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Renders the long-form "More about me" page.
  - Uses the same main layout and card styles as the resume.
  - Structured into thematic sections (education, faith, work, interests).
  - Text is intentionally written in a personal, narrative style.
-->

<main class="resume-layout">
  <section class="card about-card">
    <header class="about-header">
      <h1>More about me</h1>
      <p class="project-description">
        A more personal look at how my background, work experience and interests
        connect with my path into software development.
      </p>

      <!-- Back button to the main resume view -->
      <a href="/resume" class="btn-secondary about-back-button">
        ← Back to resume
      </a>
    </header>

    <!-- School & Education -->
    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>Learning and Education</h2>
      <p>
        School was not always the easiest place for me. Language, spelling and
        grammar have always taken more effort than they seem to take for many
        other people, and because of that expectations around my education were
        not always very high. I learned early that if I wanted to move forward, I
        had to find my own way of learning, keep going when things were slow, and
        build confidence through practical results.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is one reason why software development fits me better than I first
        expected. Programming rewards patience, structure and the willingness to
        test an idea until it works. I like that a problem can look confusing at
        the beginning, but if I break it down into smaller parts, read carefully
        and try step by step, it becomes understandable. My current B.Sc. studies
        in Software Development through BYU–Idaho give this practical curiosity a
        stronger technical foundation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Service & Responsibility -->
    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>Service and Responsibility</h2>
      <p>
        A big part of my life has been volunteer service. From 2010 to 2012 I
        lived in the USA for two years, mostly in Utah and Wyoming. I had to work
        with many different people, adapt to new places, organise my daily work,
        report on progress and learn to communicate clearly in English. Looking
        back, that time shaped me strongly, not only personally but also
        professionally.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over the years I also took on different responsibilities in my community:
        organising meetings, supporting decisions, teaching, training others,
        helping young people and working with adults who wanted to become more
        independent in difficult situations. These roles taught me planning,
        patience, public speaking, teamwork and the ability to explain things in a
        way that different people can understand. Those are skills I want to bring
        into software teams as well.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Professional Path -->
    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>Professional Path</h2>
      <p>
        My work life started in very practical environments. At McDonald’s I
        learned speed, reliability and training new people while the pressure was
        high. At Interspar I learned customer service, product quality, inventory
        work and how important clear routines are in daily operations. At Heinrich
        Reiter GmbH I grew into a warehouse team lead role, where I work with
        stock, orders, processes and people every day.
      </p>
      <p>
        This background matters for software development because I have seen
        real workflows from the inside. I know how small process improvements can
        save time, reduce mistakes and make work easier for a team. When I build
        software now, I naturally think about the people who will use it, the data
        they depend on and the practical problem the tool is supposed to solve.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also discovered that I enjoy teaching and explaining. During a forklift
        course in 2017 I helped another participant understand the content by
        translating and simplifying it. That experience stayed with me and later I
        completed a trainer qualification. Even though I did not move into a
        training career, this part of me is still important: I like helping people
        understand systems, whether that system is a warehouse process or a piece
        of code.
      </p>
      <p>
        In April 2024 I started my Software Development studies. Since then I have
        built projects with backend APIs, databases, React/TypeScript apps,
        browser game logic, deployment workflows and automated tests. These
        projects helped me discover where I currently feel strongest: structured
        problem solving, data-driven backend work and practical tools that solve
        a real user need. I know I am still growing, but I also know that I am
        consistent, curious and not afraid of hard problems.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Interests & Personality -->
    <section class="about-section">
      <h2>Interests and Personality</h2>
      <p>
        Outside of work and study I am a family person, a sports fan and someone
        who likes games with good stories and interesting systems. I played
        football as a teenager and later American football, and I still enjoy
        following sports when I have time. Gaming is another interest that stayed
        with me, especially games where decisions, story and mechanics work
        together.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also like building and repairing things. At home I try to fix many
        things myself and I have built furniture instead of simply buying it. That
        same mindset is part of why I enjoy programming: I like understanding how
        something works, improving it and seeing a real result at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        If I had to describe myself in a few words, I would say: hard-working,
        resilient, curious, practical and team-oriented. I do not come from a
        straight technical career path, but I bring responsibility, life
        experience and a strong desire to keep learning.
      </p>
    </section>
  </section>
</main>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>
